diff --git a/_posts/2026-10-13-a-ready-server-may-have-forgotten-your-cluster.md b/_posts/2026-10-13-a-ready-server-may-have-forgotten-your-cluster.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..95c647d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_posts/2026-10-13-a-ready-server-may-have-forgotten-your-cluster.md
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+---
+layout: post
+title: "A ready server may have forgotten your cluster"
+description: "Trace active state ownership before using a healthy HTTP response as evidence that an orchestrator recovered its workloads."
+category: Reliability
+book_id: inside-orch
+chapter_slug: 06-follow-one-request-through-the-control-plane
+illustration: package
+tags: [orchestration, recovery, persistence, Go, readiness]
+---
+
+An operator restarts a control-plane process. The HTTP endpoint answers, the application still serves requests, and PostgreSQL is reachable. Those observations look reassuring. They answer three separate questions, however: whether an HTTP handler is available, whether a workload remains alive, and whether a database accepts connections.
+
+They leave the recovery question open: does the new process know which services, versions, tasks, and agents it owns?
+
+## Find the owner of active state
+
+In the pinned orch source, normal `orch-server` startup constructs `controlplane.NewMemoryService`. The surrounding configuration accepts a database URL, and the repository contains a PostgreSQL store. Neither fact changes that constructor. The active implementation remains the memory service selected by the executable. See the [server composition root](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/cmd/orch-server/main.go#L142-L149).
+
+This is a useful source-review technique for any service with interchangeable backends. Begin at startup, follow the concrete value supplied to handlers and controllers, and identify where its records live. Reading a persistence interface alone cannot establish that production requests reach it.
+
+The distinction also changes backup planning. A PostgreSQL backup protects the records written to PostgreSQL. It cannot capture live records that this executable keeps elsewhere. State ownership has to be established before writing a recovery runbook around a database restore.
+
+## Give readiness a precise meaning
+
+At this snapshot, orch's `/readyz` handler returns a fixed `ready` response and a current timestamp. It does not reconstruct missing services, compare persisted task identities, or prove that agent credentials survived a restart. Read the [actual readiness handler](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/api/api.go#L440-L442).
+
+A readiness result can still be useful within that scope. The mistake is assigning it a larger meaning than its checks establish. A deployment gate should name the conditions it requires: API availability, durable state availability, controller authority, or successful workload recovery. Each needs its own evidence.
+
+For an orchestrator, an existing container is another independent observation. Docker can keep a workload running while its control plane loses the records needed to manage it. A responding application does not reveal whether the new server can scale that service or resume its interrupted rollout.
+
+## Design the experiment around preserved identity
+
+Use a disposable source-pinned environment. Before restarting the server, record a service identity, its version, task identities, and the expected node relationship. After a fresh process starts, query those same identities. Creating replacement objects after restart would conceal the failure you intend to investigate.
+
+Then check the management path: can the server recognize the existing task, authorize its agent report, and continue the intended operation? Keep application reachability in a separate observation column. An answer can be positive for traffic and negative for orchestration ownership.
+
+The book’s recorded experiment starts two distinct server processes and queries the same service and node identities after restart. Their records disappear while the default namespace is recreated. A separate Docker experiment keeps a run-owned container alive through the restart. Those observations establish process-local state loss and runtime survival in that disposable environment; they do not establish automatic container adoption. See the [worked restart laboratory](/books/inside-orch/companion/labs/01-initialization-restart/README.html).
+
+## A persistence change needs a recovery contract
+
+A durable backend must support the domain operations the API and controllers actually use, including later workload features and credentials. Replacing a constructor without checking interface coverage, transaction behavior, and restart recovery leaves important questions unanswered.
+
+Write the acceptance test before calling the integration complete. Terminate the process after a committed mutation, restart it, and verify the same logical objects. Add a case where Docker created a container before its status report arrived. Stable identity and repeatable reconciliation become essential when the outcome of an external effect is uncertain.
+
+A green endpoint earns its documented meaning. Recovery earns a stronger claim only after the system demonstrates preserved ownership across a real process boundary.
diff --git a/_posts/2026-10-13-an-offline-node-is-not-proof-that-its-workload-stopped.md b/_posts/2026-10-13-an-offline-node-is-not-proof-that-its-workload-stopped.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8c781ed
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_posts/2026-10-13-an-offline-node-is-not-proof-that-its-workload-stopped.md
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+---
+layout: post
+title: "An offline node is not proof that its workload stopped"
+description: "Heartbeat expiry supports a replacement decision, but preventing a disconnected workload from continuing requires a separate authority boundary."
+category: Reliability
+book_id: inside-orch
+chapter_slug: 19-offline-nodes-draining-and-rescheduling
+illustration: network
+tags: [orchestration, heartbeats, partitions, fencing, recovery]
+---
+
+A worker stops sending heartbeats. The control plane marks it offline and schedules replacement work elsewhere. Meanwhile, the old worker still reaches the application database and its container keeps running.
+
+Both observations can be true. Losing communication with the controller does not establish that a workload lost power, runtime access, or permission to affect another system.
+
+## Read what the timeout changes
+
+In the pinned orch implementation, `MarkStaleNodesOffline` compares heartbeat timestamps with a cutoff. It updates eligible nodes to offline, records lost-task state, emits an event, and reconciles services when nodes have expired. This is a control-plane state transition. See the [timeout implementation](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/controlplane/controlplane.go#L670-L712).
+
+The code does not establish that Docker on an unreachable worker stopped a container before the replacement decision. That is a different fact requiring another observation or mechanism.
+
+This distinction is especially important for side effects. In an illustrative report-processing service, an old and replacement instance could both reach the system receiving reports. Whether duplicate work occurs depends on that application's coordination and idempotency contract; a missed heartbeat alone does not resolve it.
+
+## Inspect the stateful exception
+
+orch's lost-task handling marks active tasks with a `NodeLost` condition. For services not marked stateful, it also changes desired state to removed and actual state to failed, with a `node_lost` reason. Stateful services retain their task state rather than following that same automatic replacement path. See [markNodeLostTasksLocked](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/controlplane/controlplane.go#L2073-L2093).
+
+That exception is useful evidence of a cautious replacement policy. It does not create replicated storage, revoke an old writer's database credentials, or prove exclusive access to data. Keeping the uncertain task visible and enforcing exclusive write authority are separate responsibilities.
+
+The incident interface should reflect that uncertainty. “Controller cannot reach node” conveys a known observation. “Workload definitely stopped” would require evidence the controller has not obtained through this timeout path.
+
+## Fencing belongs where authority is enforced
+
+A stronger design might use node isolation or an external resource that rejects writes from an obsolete owner. For example, a proposed monotonic ownership token can help only if the receiving system checks it before accepting each protected operation.
+
+Merely storing a token in the controller is insufficient. The disconnected workload might continue presenting an older token to a resource that never validates it. Similarly, a controller leadership lock limits controller participation; it does not automatically revoke authority from an existing application container.
+
+These are architectural options, not implemented orch fencing guarantees. Their acceptance tests must include the resource that ultimately decides whether the operation is allowed.
+
+## A drain exercises a different failure path
+
+A planned drain updates node status and reconciles workloads through an explicit control-plane operation. With a reachable worker, task removal can flow through the normal agent path. See [DrainNode](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/controlplane/controlplane.go#L994-L1022).
+
+Successful drain tests therefore do not establish partition safety. A disconnected agent cannot necessarily fetch its new desired task state. In orch, assigned-task reconciliation follows a successful heartbeat response in the [agent loop](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/agent/agent.go#L155-L175).
+
+## Observe both sides of the partition
+
+For a disposable experiment, interrupt only worker-to-controller communication while keeping the workload's external destination reachable. Record the server's node/task state and independently observe whether the old workload still acts. Then inspect replacement behavior, recovery after connectivity returns, and residual containers.
+
+Those are proposed observation criteria, not recorded test results. A useful recovery claim names the scope proved: detection, replacement, eventual cleanup, or rejection of stale work. It earns stronger exclusivity language only when the final authority boundary has been tested.
diff --git a/_posts/2026-10-13-follow-a-resource-limit-all-the-way-to-docker.md b/_posts/2026-10-13-follow-a-resource-limit-all-the-way-to-docker.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4449804
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_posts/2026-10-13-follow-a-resource-limit-all-the-way-to-docker.md
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+---
+layout: post
+title: "Follow a resource limit all the way to Docker"
+description: "A valid resource field and a tested scheduler do not prove that a running container receives the corresponding CPU and memory controls."
+category: Verification
+book_id: inside-orch
+chapter_slug: 05-service-specifications-resources-and-ports
+illustration: queue
+tags: [Docker, resources, CPU, memory, scheduling, verification]
+---
+
+A deployment declares CPU and memory. Validation accepts it, the scheduler has capacity tests, and the Docker adapter knows how to set limits. It is tempting to conclude that the container will receive those controls.
+
+The conclusion depends on a chain of transformations. A field can be understood at several layers and still disappear before the final runtime request.
+
+## Separate three resource questions
+
+Consider a hypothetical service with three replicas, each requesting 500 millicores and 256 MiB. Its requested aggregate is 1,500 millicores and 768 MiB. That arithmetic describes capacity accounting. A larger declared limit describes a different intended boundary for each running container.
+
+Parsing answers whether the values are valid. Placement answers whether a chosen node satisfies the planner's admission rules. Runtime enforcement answers what Docker actually configures. Evidence for one question does not automatically settle the others.
+
+orch represents requests and limits in [ResourceRequirements](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/pkg/types/types.go#L516-L519). The names establish the domain shape. To establish enforcement, follow those values out of that shape and into the container-creation call.
+
+## Check the adapter and its caller
+
+The Docker adapter's conversion is concrete: `Memory` comes from `spec.Resources.Memory`, and `NanoCPUs` comes from `spec.Resources.CPUMilli * 1_000_000`. A supplied value can therefore become Docker host configuration. See [dockerContainerConfig](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/docker/docker.go#L412-L451).
+
+Now inspect the active agent. Its `ensureTask` method constructs a `ContainerSpec` with image, task identity, environment, ports, security context, command, and volume mounts. In this pinned source, that literal does not populate `Resources`. The adapter's supported feature is consequently not evidence that ordinary service containers receive their declared limits through this path. See the [agent's create call](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/agent/agent.go#L253-L266).
+
+There is an earlier transport boundary too. The [AgentTask response type](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/controlplane/controlplane.go#L130-L137) carries several workload fields but has no explicit resource requirements field. A complete repair must inspect the whole contract rather than adding an assignment at the final call and assuming the required data exists.
+
+## Verify which placement path your service uses
+
+A similar question applies to scheduling. The standalone planner has meaningful constraint logic. Normal MemoryService service reconciliation can create tasks already assigned to ready nodes. Those paths require separate analysis; planner tests alone cannot prove that an ordinary service followed the planner.
+
+The [service reconciliation method](https://github.com/alekpopovic/orch/blob/cbfc94ade5b57e449d6b73bf1485b1c5b1647022/internal/controlplane/controlplane.go#L2249-L2311) makes that distinction visible. Describe guarantees according to the path actually executed, including any checks that path performs. Avoid turning a useful library invariant into a system-wide capacity promise.
+
+## Use two complementary acceptance tests
+
+A focused test should capture the `ContainerSpec` the agent passes to its runtime and compare its resource values with the assigned workload contract. This identifies missing propagation without requiring a live Docker daemon.
+
+A disposable Docker integration test should then inspect the created container's `HostConfig.Memory` and `HostConfig.NanoCpus`. That proves the final API configuration for the tested path. Workload measurements can investigate behavior under pressure, but they should remain separate from assertions about configured fields.
+
+The book records both boundaries. Its real Docker comparison observes zero memory and NanoCPU limits through the ordinary agent path. A direct call to the Docker adapter sets 128 MiB and 250 millicores, yielding 134,217,728 bytes and 250,000,000 NanoCPUs. This isolates missing propagation from adapter conversion; it does not measure performance under resource pressure. The [initialization laboratory](/books/inside-orch/companion/labs/01-initialization-restart/README.html) records the exact scope and cleanup.
+
+The practical review rule is to preserve the field's identity through parsing, storage, assignment, transport, and runtime conversion. When a link is absent, document the gap and test the eventual change at both the caller boundary and Docker boundary.
